+| Enable White Balance | bool | true | Apply the camera's colour temperature. Off = no white balance at all. |
+| Color Temperature (K) | 1500-15000 (slider 2500-10000) | 6500 | White balance in Kelvin. 3200 tungsten, 5600 daylight, 6500 neutral (**no-op**). |
+| Tint | -1 to 1 | 0 | The other axis: the green/magenta correction a temperature alone cannot make, for light off the blackbody locus (fluorescent, LED, foliage bounce). Positive magenta, negative green. **0 is neutral.** Scene-referred path only. |
+
+The semantics are camera-referred on both paths: the camera is told what to call white, so
+dialing 3200 K in a daylight scene reads **blue** - a tungsten setting used in daylight - and
+8000 K reads warm, exactly like a stills camera.
+
+**Neutral is 6500 K + tint 0**, and on the scene-referred path it is an exact identity, not an
+approximate one: the reference white is evaluated on the same Planckian locus as the setting, so
+source and destination white coincide and the matrix is the identity to the last bit.
+
+What the control actually does depends on the path.
+
+* **Scene referred**: a Bradford chromatic adaptation, a 3x3 in the working space, applied to
+  scene light before the display transform. This is the real thing - a change of illuminant, not
+  a tint laid over a finished frame - and it is why clipped highlights stay neutral instead of
+  taking on the cast. Tint only exists here.
+* **Stock path**: per-channel gains multiplied into the scene grading's colour balance,
+  luminance-normalised so exposure is unaffected; external (time-of-day) writes are adopted as
+  the new base so the per-frame multiply never compounds. 6500 K restores the effect and stands
+  down.
+
+### Grade
+
+*Scene-referred path only. ASC CDL, the interchange every grading tool speaks, applied in ACEScct
+to scene data BEFORE the output transform. All of it is live: a dragged slider moves the picture
+on the next frame.*
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Bypass Grade | bool | false | Take white balance, CDL and the look out of the chain in one click and leave only the output transform. The A/B - what the shot looks like ungraded, with the exposure and the optics unchanged. **false = the grade is live.** |
+| Slope (RGB) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | ASC CDL slope: a multiply in log - gain, the highlight end. **1 = neutral.** Applied first: `out = (in * slope + offset) ^ power`. |
+| Offset (RGB) | float3 | (0, 0, 0) | ASC CDL offset: an add in log - lift, the shadow end, and the usual place a colour cast is corrected. **0 = neutral.** |
+| Power (RGB) | float3 | (1, 1, 1) | ASC CDL power: gamma, the midtones, applied last. **1 = neutral**; below 1 opens the mids, above 1 closes them. |
+| Saturation | 0-4 (slider 0-2) | 1 | The ASC saturation extension, after the SOP triple, about the luminance of the graded value with AP1 weights. **1 = neutral**, 0 = monochrome. Because it happens in the working space and before the output transform, pushing it does not drift the hues the way a saturation slider on a finished picture does. |
+
+The order is the ASC specification's and is not negotiable if the numbers are to round-trip with
+Resolve: slope, then offset, then power on the clamped result, per channel with no cross-channel
+term; saturation is the ASC's own extension and comes after.
+
+### Look
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| LMT File | path | *(empty)* | Creative look as a Resolve `.cube` in ACEScct - the Look Modification Transform. Applied after the CDL and before the output transform, so it grades scene data and not a picture. **Empty = identity: pure ACES, no house look.** See [docs/SceneReferredLook.md](docs/SceneReferredLook.md). |
+
+### Output
+
+| Property | Range | Default | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| ODT File | path | `Assets/ODT/odt_srgb_100nit_aces2_65.cube` | The output transform - THE one place in the chain that knows a display exists. The default is the ACES 2.0 SDR 100 nit sRGB view, baked from the OCIO ACES studio config by `tools/ocio-bake`. Swapping this file changes only what the monitor shows, never the scene data. Empty or unreadable = the picture goes deliberately, visibly wrong (a clamped log image) rather than quietly falling back. |
+
+Both paths are resolved through the pak system: first as given, relative to the project's asset
+directory, then under `%ENGINE%`. Install the plugin's `Assets/ODT` folder into either tree and
+the same string works in the editor and in the launcher. Parse and load errors are logged once
+per path, with the reason.
 
